Skyline Problem
✅ Problem Statement

You are given an array buildings, where:

buildings[i] = [left, right, height]


Each building is a rectangle on X-axis.

Return the skyline formed by these buildings.

Skyline must be returned as:

[x-coordinate, height]


Representing change in height.

🧩 Example
Input:
[[2,9,10], [3,7,15], [5,12,12]]


Visual:

      15
     |  |
     |  |
10  |   |
|   |   |
|   |   |     12


Output:

[[2,10], [3,15], [7,12], [12,0]]

🧠 Core Idea

This is a Sweep Line Problem.

We:

Convert each building into events:

Start event

End event

Sort events

Use Max Heap to track highest building

Whenever max height changes → add skyline point

🔥 Step 1: Convert Buildings Into Events

For building:

[left, right, height]


We create:

(left, -height)   → start event
(right, height)   → end event


Why negative height at start?

Because:
When sorting,
Start event should come before end event.

🪜 Algorithm Steps

Create event list

Sort events:

By x coordinate

If same x:
Smaller height first

Create Max Heap

Track previous max height

For each event:

If start → add to heap

If end → remove from heap

Compare with previous max

If different → add skyline point

⏱ Time Complexity

Sorting:

O(N log N)


Heap operations:

O(N log N)


Overall:

O(N log N)

☕ Java Implementation
🔹 Step 1: Main Function
import java.util.*;

class Solution {

    public List<List<Integer>> getSkyline(int[][] buildings) {

        List<int[]> events = new ArrayList<>();

🔹 Step 2: Create Events
        for (int[] b : buildings) {
            events.add(new int[]{b[0], -b[2]}); // start
            events.add(new int[]{b[1], b[2]});  // end
        }


Explanation:

Start → negative height

End → positive height

🔹 Step 3: Sort Events
        Collections.sort(events, (a, b) -> {
            if (a[0] != b[0])
                return a[0] - b[0];
            return a[1] - b[1];
        });


Sort by:

X coordinate

If tie:
smaller height first

🔹 Step 4: Max Heap
        PriorityQueue<Integer> maxHeap =
            new PriorityQueue<>(Collections.reverseOrder());

        maxHeap.add(0);

        int prevMax = 0;

        List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();


Initialize:
Heap contains 0 (ground level)

🔹 Step 5: Process Events
        for (int[] event : events) {

            int x = event[0];
            int h = event[1];

            if (h < 0) {
                maxHeap.add(-h);
            } else {
                maxHeap.remove(h);
            }

            int currMax = maxHeap.peek();

            if (currMax != prevMax) {
                result.add(Arrays.asList(x, currMax));
                prevMax = currMax;
            }
        }

        return result;
    }
}

🔍 Line-by-Line Deep Explanation
If h < 0 → Start of building
maxHeap.add(-h)


Add building height.

If h > 0 → End of building
remove(h)


Remove that building's height.

After Each Event
currMax = heap.peek()


Highest building currently active.

If:

currMax != prevMax


That means skyline changed.

Add:

[x, currMax]

🔥 Dry Run

Input:

[[2,9,10], [3,7,15], [5,12,12]]


Events Sorted:

(2,-10)
(3,-15)
(5,-12)
(7,15)
(9,10)
(12,12)


Processing:

At x=2 → height=10
At x=3 → height=15
At x=7 → drop to 12
At x=12 → drop to 0

Output:

[[2,10],[3,15],[7,12],[12,0]]

⚠️ Important Edge Cases

Multiple buildings start at same x

Multiple buildings end at same x

Nested buildings

Equal heights

Complete overlap

